::: {.doc-subject}

Plot per-subject predicted survival or cumulative-hazard curves.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/greenwood/blob/feat-aft-residuals/greenwood/viz/_altair.py#L400-L495){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-Kind.FUNCTION}
```python
viz.plot_predicted_survival(
    model,
    newdata,
    *,
    type="survival",
    times=None,
    labels=None,
    xlab="Time",
    ylab=None,
    width=500,
    height=300,
    backend="altair"
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

Draws one right-continuous step curve per row of `newdata`, using any fitted model that exposes
`predict(newdata, type=..., times=..., format=...)` returning a frame of per-subject curves —
for example `SurvivalTree`, `RandomSurvivalForest`, `ExtraSurvivalTrees`, `CoxPH`, or `CoxNet`.
This complements `plot_survival`, which draws population Kaplan-Meier curves, by visualizing how
predicted risk varies across individuals.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[model]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Any]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   A fitted estimator whose `predict` accepts `type=` and returns a `time` column plus one
    column per subject (e.g. a survival forest).

<code>[newdata]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Any]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Covariates for the subjects to plot (a dataframe or 2-D array), passed to `model.predict`.

<code>[type]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[&quot;survival&quot;]{.st}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Curve to draw: `"survival"` (default) or `"cumulative_hazard"`.

<code>[times]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Any]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Times at which to evaluate the curves. Defaults to the model's training event times.

<code>[labels]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Any]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional per-subject legend labels (one per row of `newdata`).

<code>[xlab]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[&quot;Time&quot;]{.st}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   X-axis label (default `"Time"`).

<code>[ylab]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Y-axis label. Defaults to `"Survival probability"` or `"Cumulative hazard"` by `type`.

<code>[width]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[500]{.dv}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Plot dimensions in pixels (defaults 500x300).

<code>[height]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[500]{.dv}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Plot dimensions in pixels (defaults 500x300).

<code>[backend]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[&quot;altair&quot;]{.st}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Plotting backend. Currently only `"altair"` is supported.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [altair.Chart]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   An interactive Altair chart with one colored step curve per subject.
:::

## Examples {.doc-examples}

Fit a random survival forest and plot survival curves for a few subjects:

::: {#4d070bab .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import greenwood as gw

# Load data and build a right-censored response
lung = gw.load_dataset("lung", backend="pandas").dropna(subset=["ph.ecog", "ph.karno"])
y = gw.Surv.right(lung["time"], event=(lung["status"] == 2))
cols = ["age", "sex", "ph.ecog", "ph.karno", "wt.loss"]

# Fit a forest and plot per-subject predicted survival
rsf = gw.RandomSurvivalForest(n_estimators=100, random_state=0).fit(y, lung[cols])
gw.plot_predicted_survival(rsf, lung[cols][:4])
```

